INT. SILVER STAR ROLLER RINK - AFTER CLOSING

The rink is a long amber oval of varnished maple, scuffed grey where a thousand wheels have turned. Disco light dribbles over the empty benches. The carpeted walls smell of popcorn and old rubber. A single overhead fan ticks.

NELL ABERNATHY (16, long-limbed, a cast on one wrist) sits on the bench lacing a skate with her good hand. SOLEDAD IBARRA (16, braids tied up, a jersey two sizes big) circles the floor in slow, practiced loops, trailing one hand along the rail. At the snack counter, MR. HASHIMOTO (70s, cardigan, reading glasses on his forehead) counts the till, never looking up.

She crosses the rink and stops in front of the bench. She looks down at her. She doesn't smile.

SOLEDAD
You said you'd tell me.

NELL
I know.

SOLEDAD
That was Tuesday.

NELL
It's complicated.

She pushes off again, harder this time, a loop, then another. Nell watches her go. The fan ticks. At the counter, Mr. Hashimoto slides a stack of bills into a bag and does not look up.

MR. HASHIMOTO
(to the till)
Eleven dollars short.

SOLEDAD
(skating, not stopping)
It's Nell's thing, it's always Nell's thing.

NELL
My thing?

SOLEDAD
You and your cast and your secret.

She skates past the bench, close enough that the wind of it lifts the hair on Nell's neck. Nell stands. Her laces hang loose.

NELL
You don't know what happened.

SOLEDAD
Then tell me.

NELL
I'm leaving.

The word hangs in the air. Soledad stops. The skates scrape, and the sound seems very loud. Across the rink, the till slams shut.

MR. HASHIMOTO
Girls. Lights in five.

Soledad pulls off a skate and throws it. It skids across the maple and clatters against the wall. Nell flinches, then crosses the floor in her socks, stoops, and picks the skate up. She holds it out. Soledad does not take it.
